  years  and my premiums have dramatically  increased as have my co-pays so  for the same plan over the years  in 2018 i was paying 439 a month  and my specialist co-pay was 40  in 2019 it went up to 454 a month  and my co-pay went up to 50 in 2020  i was up to 478 a month and my co-pay  was up to 70 dollars and this year  i am paying five hundred and twenty  dollars a month  and my co-pays are up to seventy five  dollars so right now  i pay 520 a month that's 62.40 for the  year  just for the coverage that's if i use  nothing i guess i get an annual physical  for that  right now uh i am paying that if i don't  avail myself of  any services but i'm aware  that for lab work like blood draws  mris cat scans i have a two thousand  dollar deductible to me  i have to pay the first two grand for  that  type of uh labs and mris and that type  of thing  before my insurance will cover anything  so i've been having some ear pain  my dentist thought it was caused by  clenching my jaw at night might be a tmj  type of thing  but it's been going on for a while so  eventually my doctor said  go see an ent to rule out other things  so i'm thinking great  i pay 520 a month for insurance i know  my specialist  co-pay is 75 i make an appointment  i get a referral i confirm this doctor  is in my network  i'm good to go with my 75 dollars  i go last week doctor looks says  things look good let's do what's called  i just want to look uh  in your throat let's do a laryngoscopy  meaning they put a camera into my throat  through the nose i don't recommend it  it's not  fun but okay it was medically what my my  doctor wanted to do  let's do an audiometry up to test  your hearing and make sure you don't  have like a eustachian tube issue  i say sure now this is all stuff that  was done  in the office so when she suggests it i  say yeah let's do it i'm here  let's let's do what you want to do so  everything looks fine it's probably  tmj from clenching at night i've got a  diagnosis fantastic i pay my 75  and i go home i now get a bill  saying that this was a thousand thirty  three dollar visit  and i'm going to owe almost another 500  and i say wait wait a second how can  this be  i paid my 520 for my insurance  the doctor is in network i had a  referral i paid my co-pay i don't have a  deductible for specialists  how do i owe another 500 well i look  closely at the bill  and it says that's right david the  office visit  was billed at 420 typical for  specialists you paid your 75.  my insurance good for them negotiated a  discount so they add another 92  and the office visit is paid but then  here we go that camera in my throat for  30 seconds  that's another 400 david the audiometry  that's another 140 the tympanometry  which is five seconds to  check the pressure in my ears that's  another fifty dollars and  lo and behold that's billed like a blood  test  that's billed like an mri so even though  i'm in a room with the doctor and she  looks in my ears with one tool  and looks in my nose with another and  then puts in a camera  the camera is a completely different  type of thing  that is like lab work or an mri or blood  work  and i need to meet my 2 000 a year  deductible which of course i haven't met  because it's march  now thankfully my insurance did me the  favor of negotiating better rates so  even though the camera normally costs  400  they've negotiated it down to 280 for me  um  the hearing tests they've negotiated  down to 110. so bottom line  in addition to my 75 copay  and paying 520 for a month of health  insurance i owe an additional 466.  in essence if i didn't have any  insurance  i could have said do you offer a cash  discount  i might have paid a little more for some  of these items  i probably can't negotiate as good a  rate as my insurance company can  but i would have avoided paying the 520  a month in premiums all along so  needless to say  i'm thinking about maybe i switched to  something called direct primary care  um and then a 